November last year on the luffini land  Reserve in the Republic of the Congo  government ministers to Great Fanfare  launching a high-profile carbon  offsetting project with French oil giant  total energies the aim to plant 40  million trees in the coming decade but  now an investigation by journalists  working for Greenpeace UK Unearthed and  the source material website and shared  exclusively with Sky News suggests the  project has come at a significant cost  for an estimated 400 farmers and their  families who own or use the land  to make way for the tree planting some  say they've been blocked from their  fields without consultation or payment  s  if you are seeing with tractors you are  chased away says Clarice parfait  the crops are not harvested it's to kill  us to send us back to being slaves again  it's left some worrying about feeding  their children  I feel like these people came to kill us  on our own land says  we're not able to go far with our  studies so we gave ourselves to  agriculture I was working the fields to  feed my children and grandchildren  a few well-established families were  paid but not very much the equivalent of  80 Pence per hectare and there doesn't  appear to have been much choice about it  anyway  documents show that by the time some had  accepted money to give up their fields  the government had already changed the  law more than a year earlier to become  the private owner of the luffini reserve  that land then quickly subleased via a  forestry consultancy to Total with  reassurances from the government that  anyone else trying to use it would be  evicted other documents show that after  total's planting scheme began the  ministry of the forest economy  acknowledged in this report a range of  other problems with the project  including complaints from unpaid  families confusion over land rights and  a lack of leadership  the Congolese government declined to  comment total energies and the forestry  consultancy they worked with defended  what they called an ambitious and  pioneering partnership  but in a statement they told Sky News  that they had recently launched an  assessment this will they said establish  a complete picture of those who are  affected by the project and will  identify a remediation action plan  including livelihood restoration  measures that comply with International  standards results will be complete and  made public in 2023  this project just won a parent example  of the human cost of offsetting  vulnerable people seemingly the most at  risk in a rapidly growing and almost  unregulated Global carbon Market  Hannah Thomas Peter Sky News
 

